## Safe prerequisites
- Preserve the original artifact hash, package/version and decompilation limitations.
- Confirm the exact researcher-owned app, current Android build and dedicated device serial.
- Use synthetic accounts, resources and canaries only; no unrelated apps or destinations.
- Start with an unrooted ordinary attacker context. ADB shell, root and authorization-changing hooks do not establish ordinary-app access.

## Controlled validation
1. Derive the exact endpoint, method and resource from an authorized redacted capture or source. An endpoint name does not establish server-side authorization failure.
2. Use two researcher-controlled accounts and a harmless synthetic resource owned by account A. Keep exact origin/method approvals and prohibit redirects to unrelated infrastructure.
3. Confirm account A can read its resource and account B can read its own resource. Then make one bounded read-only request as B for A's synthetic resource.
4. Compare the actual response content, not only the status code, against authorized and unauthorized controls. Preserve relevant role/tenant/share policies and reproducibility evidence.
5. Do not test update/delete or broad ID enumeration. Any write-impact validation requires a separate reversible synthetic-data procedure and explicit scope.

## Evidence and conclusion
Record the exact entrypoint, attacker-controlled input, security boundary, sensitive sink, observed impact and commands/test-app source actually used. Attach redacted evidence plus successful authorized and rejected unauthorized controls. Distinguish unsupported observations, negative results and reproduced impact. No outcome or attacker capability is asserted by this plan.

